      "name": "ru-non-approved",
      "preamble": "# ru-non-approved — words banned in Russian code-review prose\n\n**Status:** v2, 2026-10-08. This file is the source of truth; edit it directly.\n\nTarget style:\n- Ban slang, Cyrillized English, glued Latin+Cyrillic words and pipeline jargon that developers see.\n- Keep standard loanwords (`ru-allowed`). Identifiers, paths, env vars and error strings stay byte-identical in backticks.\n- Domain «ноги» (accounting legs) and «узел» (host, node) are allowed in their literal sense (`ru-domain`).\n\nGlued words (`guard'ом`, `prior-тред`, `error-строка`, `CI-матрица`) are not rows here. The `glued` rule catches every Latin+Cyrillic token.\n\n## Pattern syntax (column `term`)\n\n- One cell holds one or more patterns, separated by commas.\n- `стем:n` — the stem plus a noun or adjective ending (`оракул:n` → оракул, оракула, оракулом, оракулы).\n- `стем:v` — the stem plus a verb ending (`скип:v` → скипнуть, скипнут, скипает, скипан).\n- `стем:nv` — both. A bare word with no `:` matches only itself.\n- `[за,про]стем:v` — optional prefixes. The stem without a prefix also matches.\n- Several words separated by spaces form a phrase. The words must follow each other with only spaces between them.\n- `#` in a phrase matches a number (`уверенност:n #` → «уверенность 5»).\n- Matching ignores case and treats ё as е. A word matches only as a whole word, never as part of a longer word.\n- The `example` cell is «bad» → «good». A test checks that the bad side fires the row and the good side does not.\n\nKinds: `slang` | `cyrillized` | `metaphor` | `pipeline`. Every hit is a hard finding (exit 1).\n\n| term | kind | use instead | example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n",
      "columns": [
        "term",
        "kind",
        "use instead",
        "example"
      ],
